    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "\"In the summers of 1993 and 1994, a reconnaissance geochemical survey was conducted in the southwestern part of the Sleetmute quadrangle.  The study area covers about 3,700 square kilometers (1,400 square miles) and includes the Buckstock Mountains and surrounding areas. Reconnaissance drainage basin geochemical surveys are a rapid and efficient means of locating upstream areas with possible mineral deposits.  The objective of this study was to use geochemical data from stream-sediment samples, and geochemical and mineralogical data from heavy-mineral-  concentrate samples, to identify areas favorable for the presence of mineral deposits.\r\n\r\nThe most rugged topography is generally in the central part of the Buckstock Mountains where the maximum elevation is about 996 m (3,269 ft).  However, much of the terrain lying outside of the Buckstock Mountains is dominated by low rolling hills with broad, sediment-filled lowlands.  Some of the study area is swampy, especially along part of the Kuskowim River where  the minimum elevation is about 60 m (200 ft).  The region is covered with vegetation that ranges from northern latitude forests to subarctic tundra.  No roads are found in the area and access is limited to travel by air or foot.  Boat access is also possible on some of the larger rivers and creeks.\"",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
